WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:26.000
Hi, this is Robert Scheer with another edition of Scheer Intelligence and I'm bringing back

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:33.720
an old guest, one of the people I've had a number of times and it's just somebody I have

00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:41.880
such enormous admiration for and he's gone through, John Kiriakko, let me just cut to

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:42.880
this chase.

00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:49.080
John, when did you, you were recruited out of what, George Washington University to go

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:52.600
work for the CIA, what year was that?

00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:58.920
I was recruited in 1989, I started working for the CIA in the first week of January of

00:00:58.920 --> 00:00:59.920
1990.

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:02.680
And you stayed there until when?

00:01:02.680 --> 00:01:07.000
I left in the spring of 2004.

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:11.160
2004, so that's what, 15 years, 14 years?

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:15.960
Yeah, about 14 and a half, yeah, and you had, and you learned Arabic.

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:18.640
I did, they taught me Arabic, right?

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:27.160
And amazingly enough, and the reason I wanted to talk to you, I teach at USC, University

00:01:27.160 --> 00:01:35.400
of Southern California, and I showed the film, The Report, which I think is excellent

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:43.880
and it's on the Senate Intelligence Committee's report on torture.

00:01:43.880 --> 00:01:49.400
And Diane Freinstein, who was the chair of that committee, over with Republican objection

00:01:49.400 --> 00:01:54.120
but she was the head of the committee and the Democrats controlled it, and they did this

00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:59.920
study of where did the torture come from, what was it all about, and they actually

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:04.280
theoretically had the cooperation of the CIA.

00:02:04.280 --> 00:02:10.680
And they came up with a view, I put it in contrast to that Hollywood movie Zero

00:02:10.680 --> 00:02:18.680
Dark Thirty, which said that torture somehow worked and got to help the U.S. capture,

00:02:18.680 --> 00:02:24.600
have been lied and then kill them, and so forth, and none of that turned out to be true.

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:29.400
But the fact of the matter is I can't refer listeners to the torture report that they

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:30.880
paid for.

00:02:30.880 --> 00:02:33.080
How many pages was it?

00:02:33.080 --> 00:02:36.520
The report itself was about 5,500 pages long.

00:02:36.520 --> 00:02:37.520
Yeah.

00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:38.520
And when did it come out?

00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:41.120
It came out in December 2014.

00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:42.120
Okay.

00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:49.720
So, it's almost a decade ago, and the only part that was released was the introduction

00:02:49.720 --> 00:02:52.240
heavily redacted.

00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:57.600
The report itself may not even exist anymore, I don't know, there were a couple of copies

00:02:57.600 --> 00:02:58.600
right?

00:02:58.600 --> 00:02:59.600
Right.

00:02:59.600 --> 00:03:00.600
And what happened to it?

00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:01.600
Yeah.

00:03:01.600 --> 00:03:05.200
That's really a very important issue that I think most Americans either don't know

00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:06.720
or have forgotten about.

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:13.880
The CIA only produced six or seven copies, I should say, of the full report.

00:03:13.880 --> 00:03:20.640
What we saw was a heavily redacted version of the executive summary of the report, which

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:22.960
was about 500 pages.

00:03:22.960 --> 00:03:27.280
But the report itself was owned by the CIA.

00:03:27.280 --> 00:03:34.080
The CIA maintained ownership and control, final word, over the actual report itself.

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:43.080
So there was a report in the New York Times about three years after the report was finally

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:49.780
written saying that the CIA was going to begin taking back the copies that had gone to places

00:03:49.780 --> 00:03:54.680
like the Executive Office of the President, the Office of the Vice President, the Senate

00:03:54.680 --> 00:04:00.080
and House Intelligence Committees, et cetera, and they were going to begin destroying

00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:01.080
them.

00:04:01.080 --> 00:04:03.840
Well, we don't know if that ever happened.

00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:06.040
So it may exist.

00:04:06.040 --> 00:04:07.740
It may not exist.

00:04:07.740 --> 00:04:09.140
We just don't know.

00:04:09.140 --> 00:04:13.400
What we do know is that it probably will never be declassified.

00:04:13.400 --> 00:04:20.360
So the reason this is so critical is we're now getting into any conspiracy theories, although

00:04:20.360 --> 00:04:27.080
conspiracy theory is kind of a way of just alluding to hypothesis that either have

00:04:27.080 --> 00:04:31.360
been explored or have not or can be documented or not.

00:04:31.360 --> 00:04:39.720
But the fact of the matter is the main evidence that would be available about what happened on

00:04:39.720 --> 00:04:46.120
9-11, how we got into a war on terror, what Al Qaeda was all about, what was this relation

00:04:46.120 --> 00:04:53.760
to Saudi Arabia after all 15 of the 19 hijackers were Saudi troops, whatever they were, the

00:04:53.760 --> 00:04:56.720
young men from Saudi Arabia.

00:04:56.720 --> 00:05:02.680
And just this week as we're recording this, the President of the United States, who spoke

00:05:02.680 --> 00:05:07.720
at the UN about we represent democracy in just about every other country in the world

00:05:07.720 --> 00:05:14.320
who's not in Western Europe or not a form of major colonial power, represents authoritarian

00:05:14.320 --> 00:05:17.520
governments, whatever they are.

00:05:17.520 --> 00:05:21.880
And ironically, it's the same week in which he said he's going to develop an alliance

00:05:21.880 --> 00:05:25.680
with Saudi Arabia to protect them militarily.

00:05:25.720 --> 00:05:31.680
So here's this self-proclaimed great democracy, and we still really don't know the details

00:05:31.680 --> 00:05:39.520
of what happened at 9-11 as far as Saudi Arabia's relationship to certainly citizens of Saudi

00:05:39.520 --> 00:05:47.120
Arabia who were living there, actually 15 of the 19 were from there, none from Iraq,

00:05:47.120 --> 00:05:52.040
none from Afghanistan so that countries that we invaded.

00:05:52.040 --> 00:05:58.280
So you would expect that when you capture people, you would learn something about what

00:05:58.280 --> 00:06:04.120
they are, and then you would have a trial, hopefully a public trial of these people.

00:06:04.120 --> 00:06:05.840
That didn't happen.

00:06:05.840 --> 00:06:11.920
And you figure into this historic story, maybe certainly one of the major, one of

00:06:11.920 --> 00:06:17.280
the three or four most important events in American history changed a whole nature

00:06:17.280 --> 00:06:21.880
of our government, just the national security state solidified it.

00:06:21.880 --> 00:06:27.800
You were then in the CIA, you were an Arabic speaker, and let's just begin with that.

00:06:27.800 --> 00:06:34.520
You were involved in the first major capture of somebody who was said at the time to be

00:06:34.520 --> 00:06:37.440
the number three person in al-Qaeda.

00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:42.760
And in the movie, the report deals with the interrogation of that person.

00:06:42.760 --> 00:06:48.680
And the reason I felt some urgency in doing this is in my class that I was teaching and

00:06:48.680 --> 00:06:51.160
I showed the report, I was shocked.

00:06:51.160 --> 00:06:58.600
That's a class of about, you know, I think 68 students, all very sharp, great SAT scores.

00:06:58.600 --> 00:07:04.080
And none of them knew about this report, and none of them were really familiar

00:07:04.080 --> 00:07:10.920
through their high school or years of college education about what had happened,

00:07:10.920 --> 00:07:13.080
the torture, the torture program.

00:07:13.080 --> 00:07:14.080
That is shocking to me.

00:07:14.080 --> 00:07:15.080
Yeah.

00:07:15.080 --> 00:07:16.480
And here's the president of the United States talks.

00:07:16.480 --> 00:07:21.080
We are the great democracy, but we are the great democracy that did the torture

00:07:21.080 --> 00:07:22.080
program.

00:07:22.080 --> 00:07:23.640
You were there.

00:07:23.640 --> 00:07:29.440
Let's set this historically just because people don't know that story now, John.

00:07:29.440 --> 00:07:30.920
Tell us what happened.

00:07:30.920 --> 00:07:32.080
How did you get the call?

00:07:32.080 --> 00:07:37.280
Why were you the person that received this prisoner?

00:07:37.280 --> 00:07:38.280
And what happened to him?

00:07:38.280 --> 00:07:39.280
Right.

00:07:39.280 --> 00:07:43.160
And really, like so many of these stories, it begins on 9-11.

00:07:43.160 --> 00:07:49.620
Like everybody else in the CIA, I volunteered that very day to go to Afghanistan and fight

00:07:49.620 --> 00:07:52.640
or do whatever was required of me.

00:07:52.640 --> 00:08:00.120
I volunteered repeatedly, stressing that I was fluent in the Arabic language and certainly

00:08:00.120 --> 00:08:08.240
they would need people to carry out these interrogations and interviews and debriefings.

00:08:08.240 --> 00:08:10.320
And they just ignored me.

00:08:10.320 --> 00:08:14.960
Every time I would volunteer, they'd say, thank you very much, and they would ignore me.

00:08:14.960 --> 00:08:24.080
Well, it turned out in those early days immediately after 9-11, they weren't interviewing people.

00:08:24.080 --> 00:08:26.000
They weren't interrogating anybody.

00:08:26.000 --> 00:08:27.880
They were killing them.

00:08:27.880 --> 00:08:31.680
And so finally I made what really was an idle threat.

00:08:31.680 --> 00:08:36.760
I went into the office of the deputy director for counterterrorism who was a friend of

00:08:36.760 --> 00:08:44.200
mine and I said, look, if you don't send me to Afghanistan right now, I am walking straight

00:08:44.200 --> 00:08:49.200
to Exxon with my Arabic and I am not looking back.

00:08:49.200 --> 00:08:52.280
And he said, oh, relax, relax, okay.

00:08:52.280 --> 00:08:56.240
And I had been volunteering to him for months at that point.

00:08:56.240 --> 00:08:58.320
And he said, can you go to Pakistan?

00:08:58.320 --> 00:09:00.160
I said, yes, when?

00:09:00.160 --> 00:09:01.160
He said, tomorrow.

00:09:01.160 --> 00:09:02.160
I said, yes.

00:09:02.160 --> 00:09:03.160
What do you want me to do?

00:09:03.160 --> 00:09:06.360
He said, I want you to be chief of counterterrorism operations.

00:09:06.360 --> 00:09:07.920
I said, okay, done.

00:09:07.920 --> 00:09:13.280
And I walked right over to the travel office and made a reservation, called my then-girlfriend.

00:09:13.280 --> 00:09:16.320
And Langley, this is at the CIA's headquarters.

00:09:16.320 --> 00:09:18.560
At the CIA's headquarters, yeah.

00:09:18.560 --> 00:09:23.040
I called my girlfriend, who was also a CIA officer, told her I was going to Pakistan

00:09:23.040 --> 00:09:24.040
the next day.

00:09:24.040 --> 00:09:25.040
She said, how long?

00:09:25.040 --> 00:09:27.960
I said, I don't know, six months, 12 months, I don't know.

00:09:27.960 --> 00:09:30.920
She said, okay, I'll meet you at your place and help you pack.

00:09:30.920 --> 00:09:33.160
And the next day I flew to Pakistan.

00:09:33.160 --> 00:09:37.760
So we began doing counterterrorism raids very slowly.

00:09:37.760 --> 00:09:39.400
I had been there about a week.

00:09:39.400 --> 00:09:45.040
I'd come up with a standard operating procedure for taking down a suspected safe house.

00:09:45.040 --> 00:09:50.880
And in the first raid that we did, of course, we partnered with the FBI because 9-11 was

00:09:50.880 --> 00:09:53.120
still an open criminal investigation.

00:09:53.120 --> 00:09:58.080
We partnered with Pakistani intelligence because, after all, it's their country.

00:09:58.080 --> 00:10:00.000
And so we...

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:06.480
He broke down the door of this house at two o'clock in the morning and grabbed two 18-year-olds.

00:10:06.480 --> 00:10:08.560
Both of them began crying.

00:10:08.560 --> 00:10:11.040
One asked if he could call his mother.

00:10:11.040 --> 00:10:13.600
It was pathetic.

00:10:13.600 --> 00:10:15.160
And it was easy.

00:10:15.160 --> 00:10:20.720
And so about a week later, a friendly Arab intelligence officer passed me a slip of

00:10:20.720 --> 00:10:25.640
paper with an address on it, and he said, I think this is an Egyptian Islamic jihad

00:10:25.640 --> 00:10:26.640
safe house.

00:10:26.640 --> 00:10:27.640
I said, great.

00:10:27.640 --> 00:10:28.640
Thank you.

00:10:28.640 --> 00:10:33.920
We broke down the door and grabbed two members of the Egyptian Islamic jihad.

00:10:33.920 --> 00:10:36.920
So I said, you know, this is going pretty well.

00:10:36.920 --> 00:10:41.560
But we're going to be here for 50 years if we're going to be breaking down doors and

00:10:41.560 --> 00:10:44.520
grabbing one guy or two guys.

00:10:44.520 --> 00:10:46.440
So I came up with this idea.

00:10:46.440 --> 00:10:54.120
We had CIA officers all the way up and down the Pakistan-Afghanistan border in every little

00:10:54.120 --> 00:10:59.280
border village just sort of standing there with binoculars to see if there were al-Qaeda

00:10:59.280 --> 00:11:01.120
people trying to cross the border.

00:11:01.120 --> 00:11:03.120
So I said, I had this idea.

00:11:03.120 --> 00:11:06.440
Let's pull all of our guys off the border.

00:11:06.440 --> 00:11:11.440
We'll let al-Qaeda come into the country, because you know they're going to all go

00:11:11.440 --> 00:11:13.800
to the same handful of safe houses.

00:11:13.800 --> 00:11:17.920
You know they're going to make a mistake, probably an electronic mistake.

00:11:17.920 --> 00:11:22.360
And then we can raid the house and catch 20, 30, 40 of them at a time.

00:11:22.360 --> 00:11:25.520
So we did that and then started having success.

00:11:25.520 --> 00:11:38.440
Well, on the night of March 22, 2002, we carried out 13 simultaneous raids at 2 a.m.

00:11:38.440 --> 00:11:43.000
And in the course of those raids, we captured Abu Zubeda, who we believed at the time was

00:11:43.000 --> 00:11:44.840
the number three in al-Qaeda.

00:11:44.840 --> 00:11:51.120
We captured the commanders of both of al-Qaeda's training camps in Kandahar and Helmand

00:11:51.120 --> 00:11:53.480
provinces in southern Afghanistan.

00:11:53.480 --> 00:11:59.320
And we caught dozens upon dozens of low-level al-Qaeda fighters.

00:11:59.320 --> 00:12:03.320
Now you said something that's important.

00:12:03.320 --> 00:12:07.040
You talked about Guantanamo for a moment, and I want to tell you about the first time

00:12:07.040 --> 00:12:09.520
I heard anything about Guantanamo.

00:12:09.520 --> 00:12:14.760
I mean I knew since I was a kid that Guantanamo was this American base that we maintained

00:12:14.760 --> 00:12:17.560
in Cuba that the Cubans hated.

00:12:17.560 --> 00:12:19.520
And that was pretty much all I knew about it.

00:12:19.520 --> 00:12:25.700
So after the Abu Zubeda raids, we had captured so many people that my Pakistani intelligence

00:12:25.700 --> 00:12:30.000
counterpart called me one day and said, listen, the jail is full.

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:33.400
We just can't squeeze one more person into the jail.

00:12:33.400 --> 00:12:36.720
You're going to have to do something with all these prisoners.

00:12:36.720 --> 00:12:40.360
So I called CIA headquarters, the Counterterrorism Center.

00:12:40.360 --> 00:12:44.520
And I said, Raul Pindi, jail is full, and the Pakistanis want us to get these guys

00:12:44.520 --> 00:12:45.520
out.

00:12:45.520 --> 00:12:46.680
What should I do with them?

00:12:46.680 --> 00:12:53.600
And the response was put them on a C-12 transport plane and send them to Guantanamo.

00:12:53.600 --> 00:12:56.500
And I said, Guantanamo, Cuba?

00:12:56.500 --> 00:12:57.900
And he said, yes.

00:12:57.900 --> 00:13:01.400
I said, why in the world would we send them to Cuba?

00:13:01.400 --> 00:13:03.760
And he said, oh, we came up with this idea.

00:13:03.760 --> 00:13:08.520
We're going to send everybody to Cuba for a couple of weeks until we can decide

00:13:08.520 --> 00:13:11.320
which federal district court to try them in.

00:13:11.320 --> 00:13:14.280
You don't mean actually the country of Cuba.

00:13:14.280 --> 00:13:20.400
I mean, a portion of Cuba that the US had seized through its colonial connection.

00:13:20.400 --> 00:13:21.400
Right.

00:13:21.400 --> 00:13:22.400
Guantanamo.

00:13:22.400 --> 00:13:23.400
Yeah.

00:13:23.400 --> 00:13:24.400
Yeah.

00:13:24.400 --> 00:13:25.400
But it's in Cuba.

00:13:25.400 --> 00:13:26.400
Yeah.

00:13:26.400 --> 00:13:27.400
Yeah.

00:13:27.400 --> 00:13:28.400
But it was obviously controlled by the US.

00:13:28.400 --> 00:13:29.400
Right.

00:13:29.400 --> 00:13:30.400
Yeah.

00:13:30.400 --> 00:13:31.400
It's US base in Cuba.

00:13:31.400 --> 00:13:39.080
So the idea was to divide them up and try them in either Boston, the Southern

00:13:39.080 --> 00:13:44.800
or Eastern districts of New York, the Eastern District of Virginia, or the Federal District

00:13:44.800 --> 00:13:48.440
of Washington, D.C. I said, that's a great idea.

00:13:48.440 --> 00:13:54.400
And so we started loading people onto plane after plane after plane and sent them to

00:13:54.400 --> 00:13:55.400
Guantanamo.

00:13:55.400 --> 00:14:00.360
In the end, we sent something like 770 people to Guantanamo.

00:14:00.360 --> 00:14:04.960
It turned out that the vice president at the time, Dick Cheney, didn't have any intention

00:14:04.960 --> 00:14:10.000
of ever allowing any of those people to go on trial in the United States.

00:14:10.000 --> 00:14:16.640
And then later on, of course, Congress acted and formally banned the transfer of any prisoner

00:14:16.640 --> 00:14:21.520
from Guantanamo to the United States, even to face trial, or to be transferred into

00:14:21.520 --> 00:14:23.320
a US prison.

00:14:23.320 --> 00:14:26.600
So that just never happened.

00:14:26.600 --> 00:14:32.200
And so one of the prisoners who is still there is the man you captured.

00:14:32.200 --> 00:14:33.200
Yes.

00:14:33.200 --> 00:14:34.200
Abu Zabeda.

00:14:35.200 --> 00:14:41.000
And for people who haven't watched it, I'm trying to encourage people to watch the report.

00:14:41.000 --> 00:14:45.400
Annette Benning is in it.

00:14:45.400 --> 00:14:46.400
Help me here.

00:14:46.400 --> 00:14:48.400
It's a dramatic movie.

00:14:48.400 --> 00:14:51.520
Adam Driver is the star.

00:14:51.520 --> 00:14:55.040
Annette Benning is terrific as Diane Feinstein.

00:14:55.040 --> 00:14:59.400
I have to tell you, I didn't watch this film when it first came out because I was

00:14:59.480 --> 00:15:06.080
afraid that it would upset me, it would make my heart race, it would give me PTSD or something.

00:15:06.080 --> 00:15:10.640
I ended up watching it on a plane, and I couldn't tear myself away from it.

00:15:10.640 --> 00:15:13.600
It was so wonderfully done.

00:15:13.600 --> 00:15:17.840
And the point they made, though, in this film, which I've heard you make, is that

00:15:17.840 --> 00:15:24.920
when you were in charge at that time, and then they brought in the FBI experienced

00:15:24.920 --> 00:15:27.560
person who had to do interrogation.

00:15:27.560 --> 00:15:33.360
You actually got the only useful information that was ever obtained in that process.

00:15:33.360 --> 00:15:34.360
That's right.

00:15:34.360 --> 00:15:39.600
And when the torture guys from the CIA took over and they excluded the FBI when the FBI

00:15:39.600 --> 00:15:43.760
pulled out because they wouldn't do torture, they didn't get any more information.

00:15:43.760 --> 00:15:49.040
And that prisoner, that prisoner without trial, who turns out not to have actually

00:15:49.040 --> 00:15:52.680
been officially a member of Al Qaeda, is still in Guantanamo.

00:15:52.680 --> 00:15:53.680
Yeah.

00:15:54.080 --> 00:15:58.680
This is a big problem for a lot of different reasons.

00:15:58.680 --> 00:16:05.480
Abu Zubaydah was first taken to a secret prison where he was interrogated by an FBI

00:16:05.480 --> 00:16:08.480
officer named Ali Soufan.

00:16:08.480 --> 00:16:13.880
Ali was a terrific translator, an experienced interrogator.

00:16:13.880 --> 00:16:18.280
And over the course of many weeks, something like six weeks, he established

00:16:18.280 --> 00:16:21.480
a rapport with Abu Zubaydah, a relationship that allowed them to

00:16:21.480 --> 00:16:25.560
carry on a normal human-to-human conversation.

00:16:25.560 --> 00:16:31.480
And in the course of that conversation, Abu Zubaydah gave us information that we had

00:16:31.480 --> 00:16:36.120
never been able to collect before at the CIA or at the FBI.

00:16:36.120 --> 00:16:40.160
He gave us, for example, the Al Qaeda wiring diagram.

00:16:40.160 --> 00:16:45.120
Now that may not sound like a big deal, but I assure you it's a very big deal

00:16:45.120 --> 00:16:50.640
because we knew about Osama bin Laden and Aiman Isawahdi, and we really didn't

00:16:50.680 --> 00:16:52.440
know anything else.

00:16:52.440 --> 00:16:58.920
So one of the examples that I like to give of what I mean by the wiring diagram

00:16:58.920 --> 00:17:06.480
is Ali asked Abu Zubaydah, if you were going to do an operation in Düsseldorf,

00:17:06.480 --> 00:17:07.840
how would you do that?

00:17:07.840 --> 00:17:12.480
And Abu Zubaydah responded that, well, there's this guy, Mohamed, and he's

00:17:12.480 --> 00:17:16.200
at this address, and Mohamed is good at organization.

00:17:16.200 --> 00:17:19.320
And he has a friend, Ahmed, and here's Ahmed's phone number.

00:17:19.320 --> 00:17:21.360
And Ahmed has access to weapons.

00:17:21.360 --> 00:17:23.760
And there's another guy, Abdul Rahman.

00:17:23.760 --> 00:17:24.880
He's Ahmed's cousin.

00:17:24.880 --> 00:17:26.880
He has access to explosives.

00:17:26.880 --> 00:17:31.680
So then we could call the Germans and say, listen, you have a problem in Düsseldorf.

00:17:31.680 --> 00:17:33.480
And here's the information.

00:17:33.480 --> 00:17:35.640
And they could wrap up that cell.

00:17:35.640 --> 00:17:38.280
Well, we didn't know that these cells existed.

00:17:38.280 --> 00:17:43.000
We suspected that they did, but we didn't have definitive information

00:17:43.000 --> 00:17:47.480
and especially lead information as to how to locate and capture them.

00:17:47.480 --> 00:17:49.280
Abu Zubaydah gave us that.

00:17:49.280 --> 00:17:53.480
Abu Zubaydah also identified to us Khalid Sheikh Mohamed

00:17:53.480 --> 00:17:56.800
as the mastermind of the 9-11 attacks.

00:17:56.800 --> 00:18:00.720
We didn't know that name until Abu Zubaydah gave it to us.

00:18:00.720 --> 00:18:05.080
We knew that there was a very bad man out there somewhere who went

00:18:05.080 --> 00:18:07.640
by the nom de guerre, Muhtar.

00:18:07.640 --> 00:18:12.960
Muhtar was the mastermind of what was called the Bojinka operation.

00:18:13.000 --> 00:18:18.600
It was a plan to hijack 6, 747s from Manila

00:18:18.600 --> 00:18:24.840
and fly them into buildings up and down the west coast of the United States.

00:18:24.840 --> 00:18:31.560
The operation ever took place because a housekeeper stumbled on the plans

00:18:31.560 --> 00:18:37.160
while cleaning the apartment of one of the would-be hijackers.

00:18:37.160 --> 00:18:40.080
And she called the police and the police raided the apartment

00:18:40.080 --> 00:18:42.120
and then it just never happened.

00:18:42.120 --> 00:18:45.600
So we knew this Muhtar was out there planning terrible things.

00:18:45.600 --> 00:18:47.000
We didn't know who he was.

00:18:47.000 --> 00:18:52.640
Abu Zubaydah was amazed that we didn't know that Muhtar was Khalid Sheikh Mohamed.

00:18:52.640 --> 00:18:57.120
And so once he told us who that was, we could target Khalid Sheikh Mohamed

00:18:57.120 --> 00:19:03.640
and we ended up capturing him in Rawalpindi, Pakistan in August of 2002.

00:19:05.560 --> 00:19:10.240
An interesting thing, you say they didn't know Khalid Sheikh Mohamed,

00:19:10.280 --> 00:19:16.360
but he had been a student, I believe in North Carolina, an engineering student

00:19:16.360 --> 00:19:20.520
and actually kind of a normal person.

00:19:20.520 --> 00:19:24.160
Lived with an American family when he was a student in North Carolina.

00:19:24.160 --> 00:19:27.760
Somehow I'd gotten radicalized after that,

