but was actually knowledgeable about American culture and so forth.

00:19:32.320 --> 00:19:34.720
Oh, yes. Yeah, very much so.

00:19:34.720 --> 00:19:43.120
So you came up with this information and then you discovered that

00:19:43.120 --> 00:19:47.960
he wasn't actually Abu Zubaydah, was not actually a member of al-Qaeda.

00:19:47.960 --> 00:19:51.120
Right, that was one of several surprises for us.

00:19:51.120 --> 00:19:54.080
He had never formally joined al-Qaeda.

00:19:54.080 --> 00:19:56.800
He had never pledged fealty to Osama bin Laden.

00:19:56.800 --> 00:20:00.000
He had never sworn an oath, so he was not the number

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:05.500
three in Al Qaeda. He was a bad man, and he did a lot of work on behalf of Al Qaeda, but

00:20:05.500 --> 00:20:12.520
he was never a member. Now, getting back to Ali Sufran, the CIA was jealous, frankly,

00:20:12.520 --> 00:20:18.260
at the progress that Ali was making. And every day, at the end of the day, he would

00:20:18.260 --> 00:20:23.380
write a cable back to FBI headquarters recounting the conversation that he had had that

00:20:23.380 --> 00:20:31.940
day with Abu Zabeda. Well, even post-911, the CIA and the FBI hated each other so

00:20:31.940 --> 00:20:39.420
intensely that even their computer systems were not compatible. And so, Ali is

00:20:39.420 --> 00:20:43.820
writing these cables back to headquarters, back to FBI headquarters every day, and the

00:20:43.820 --> 00:20:51.340
CIA is not seeing them. Once in a while, the FBI would write a memo and send it

00:20:51.340 --> 00:20:56.700
over to the CIA saying, yeah, we're talking to him, so here's a memo. Well, the CIA was

00:20:56.700 --> 00:21:03.460
upset. This is a very serious bureaucratic kerfuffle between the two organizations.

00:21:03.460 --> 00:21:10.660
And so, in late July of 2002, George Tenant, who was the CIA's director at the time,

00:21:10.660 --> 00:21:18.540
went to President Bush and asked President Bush to turn primacy of the case over to

00:21:18.540 --> 00:21:25.220
the CIA to take it away from the FBI and give it to the CIA. Why George W. Bush agreed

00:21:25.220 --> 00:21:29.420
to do that is something that I don't think we'll ever understand. He's never talked

00:21:29.420 --> 00:21:33.900
about it. It wasn't in the torture report, and George Tenant has never talked

00:21:33.900 --> 00:21:39.260
about it, but that's what happened. And so, the FBI knew that the CIA was going

00:21:39.260 --> 00:21:45.180
to begin torturing him. And so, to protest that, the FBI withdrew all of

00:21:45.220 --> 00:21:50.340
its personnel, not just from the secret site, but from the country that the secret

00:21:50.340 --> 00:21:56.700
site was in. On August the 2nd, 2002, the CIA began torturing Abu Zubaydah, and he

00:21:56.700 --> 00:22:05.540
immediately clammed up. He was so upset, so angry, and disgusted when they

00:22:05.540 --> 00:22:12.780
started torturing him that he just went silent. But the two contract

00:22:12.780 --> 00:22:18.980
psychologists, James Mitchell and Bruce Jessen, to whom the CIA had paid $108

00:22:18.980 --> 00:22:22.940
million to come up with the torture program, had to prove that it was

